Cap the length of one line read by mailstream_read_line*().

mailstream_read_line_append grew the line buffer until a newline arrived, so a hostile or broken server (or a
MITM on a plaintext connection) could exhaust memory with one endless line. struct mailstream gets
max_line_length (default MAILSTREAM_DEFAULT_MAX_LINE_LENGTH, 64 MiB, the same as the IMAP literal limit;
0 = no limit) with mailstream_set/get_max_line_length(); a longer line fails the read (NULL).

diff -ruN a/src/data-types/mailstream.c b/src/data-types/mailstream.c
--- a/src/data-types/mailstream.c
+++ b/src/data-types/mailstream.c
@@ -83,6 +83,7 @@
   
   s->logger = NULL;
   s->logger_context = NULL;
+  s->max_line_length = MAILSTREAM_DEFAULT_MAX_LINE_LENGTH;
   
   mailstream_set_low(s, low);
   
@@ -420,3 +421,13 @@
   }
   carray_free(certificate_chain);
 }
+
+void mailstream_set_max_line_length(mailstream * s, size_t max_line_length)
+{
+  s->max_line_length = max_line_length;
+}
+
+size_t mailstream_get_max_line_length(mailstream * s)
+{
+  return s->max_line_length;
+}
